layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | Everything you can type: the slash commands inside Claude Code, Buildyboy's own command-line tool, and the
    | installer options. Commands that mention your server already use this server's address.

  h2#slash Slash commands in Claude Code
  p These come with the Buildyboy plugin and work in any Claude Code session on a paired machine. Claude prints their answer as is.
  dl
    dt
      code /bb-status
    dd
      | Checks the connection and shows the server, this machine's name, whether walk mode is on for this session, how
      | many bytes are waiting in the local spool, and this session's link. If the server cannot be reached it says so;
      | your session keeps working.
    dt
      code /bb-walk
      | [
      code on
      | |
      code off
      | |
      code 5m
      | ]
    dd
      | Walk mode for this session: on, off, or on with a wait of N minutes (0 to 9) at the end of each turn. See
      a(href="/docs/walk-mode#bb-walk") Walk mode
      | .
    dt
      code /bb-link
    dd
      | Prints this session's address, for example
      code {{ site.url }}/s/1b2c…
      | . Open it on your phone to follow along.
  p
    | If the machine is not paired, all three say "Buildyboy is not configured on this machine" and point you to the
    | Machines page.

  h2#bb The bb command-line tool
  p
    | Buildyboy's tool lives in the code itself, as
    code cli/bb.ts
    | . Run it with Node.js 24 from a copy of the code, as
    code node cli/bb.ts <command>
    | . It is not added to your PATH, so the examples below always use the full form.
  dl
    dt#doctor
      code node cli/bb.ts doctor
    dd
      | Checks a machine: whether
      code ~/.buildyboy/config.json
      | exists, whether the server is reachable, whether it accepts this machine's key, how much is in the spool, and
      | whether the hook has recently backed off after a failure. Each line starts with ✔ or ✘.
    dt#pair
      code node cli/bb.ts pair <server> <code> [name]
    dd
      | Pairs this machine with a code from the Machines page and writes
      code ~/.buildyboy/config.json
      | . The name defaults to the computer's host name, with
      code -wsl
      | added inside WSL. It does not install the plugin; see
      a(href="/docs/machines#by-hand") Pairing without the installer
      | .
    dt#runner-cmd
      code node cli/bb.ts runner [--config <file>]
    dd
      | Starts the runner in this window. Reads
      code ~/.buildyboy/runner.json
      | unless you give another file. See
      a(href="/docs/runner") Setting up the runner
      | .
    dt#import
      code node cli/bb.ts import [--since <days>] [--dir <folder>]
    dd
      | Sends Claude Code transcripts from
      code ~/.claude/projects
      | to the server. See
      a(href="/docs/insights#import") Importing old history
      | .
    dt#set-password
      code node cli/bb.ts set-password
    dd
      | Run on the server. Sets the web password (at least 8 characters). It reads the password from the
      code BB_PASSWORD
      | variable or from its input, so the easiest form is to pipe it in. There is an example just below this list.
    dt#backup
      code node cli/bb.ts backup [--out <file>]
    dd
      | Run on the server. Writes a consistent copy of the database, by default into the data folder's
      code backups
      | folder, and keeps the newest 14 there.
    dt#token
      code node cli/bb.ts token <name>
    dd
      | Run on the server. Creates a machine and prints its id and key, for setting up a machine completely by hand.
      | Pairing codes are easier.
    dt#server-cmd
      code node cli/bb.ts server
    dd Starts the server in this window (the same as npm start). Normally the server runs as a service instead.
  p Setting the password on the standard WSL install:
  pre
    code
      | cd ~/apps/buildyboy
      | echo 'my-long-password' | BUILDYBOY_DATA=~/.local/share/buildyboy node cli/bb.ts set-password
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | On the server, commands that touch the database need
        code BUILDYBOY_DATA
        | pointing at the real data folder. Without it they quietly use a new, empty database in the code folder. Setting
        | the password this way does not log out browsers that are already logged in; change it in Settings for that.

  h2#installers Installer options
  h3#install-sh install.sh (Linux, macOS, WSL)
  pre
    code
      | curl -fsSL {{ site.url }}/install.sh | bash -s -- PAIRCODE [--name NAME] [--runner]
      | curl -fsSL {{ site.url }}/install.sh | bash -s -- --uninstall [--yes]
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Option
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Or variable
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Meaning
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") PAIRCODE
          td(data-part="td") BB_CODE
          td(data-part="td") The pairing code. Leave it out on an already paired machine to just refresh the plugin.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") --name NAME
          td(data-part="td") BB_NAME
          td(data-part="td") The machine's name in Buildyboy.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") --runner
          td(data-part="td") BB_RUNNER=1
          td(data-part="td") Also install the runner as a service. Needs BB_REPO, the folder of a copy of the code.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") --uninstall
          td(data-part="td") BB_UNINSTALL=1
          td(data-part="td") Remove the plugin and the runner, and ask before deleting ~/.buildyboy.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") --yes
          td(data-part="td") BB_YES=1
          td(data-part="td") Do not ask before deleting.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") —
          td(data-part="td") BB_URL
          td(data-part="td") A different server address (default: the server the script came from).
  p
    | Variables must be set for
    code bash
    | , after the pipe:
    code curl … | BB_REPO=~/buildyboy bash -s -- --runner
    | .
  h3#install-ps1 install.ps1 (Windows)
  p
    | The Windows installer takes the same settings as variables only:
    code BB_CODE
    | ,
    code BB_NAME
    | ,
    code BB_RUNNER
    | ,
    code BB_REPO
    | ,
    code BB_UNINSTALL
    | ,
    code BB_YES
    | and
    code BB_URL
    | . Set them first, then run the script:
  pre
    code
      | $env:BB_CODE='PAIRCODE'; $env:BB_NAME='laptop'; irm {{ site.url }}/install.ps1 | iex
      | $env:BB_UNINSTALL='1'; irm {{ site.url }}/install.ps1 | iex
  p It reports problems as a red ERROR line rather than a failing exit code, so read its output.

  h2#client-env Settings on each machine
  p These variables change how the plugin, the runner and the tools behave on a machine:
  dl
    dt
      code BUILDYBOY_DISABLED=1
    dd Switches the plugin off completely for that terminal or session. Handy for a private session.
    dt
      code BUILDYBOY_URL
      | ,
      code BUILDYBOY_TOKEN
    dd Use a different server and key than the ones in config.json.
    dt
      code BUILDYBOY_HOME
    dd Use a different folder than ~/.buildyboy.
    dt
      code BUILDYBOY_STATUSLINE_NEXT
    dd Your own status line command, run by the statusline bridge.
  h3#files What is in ~/.buildyboy
  dl
    dt
      code config.json
    dd The server address, this machine's id and its key. Keep it private.
    dt
      code runner.json
    dd The runner's settings, written by you.
    dt
      code marketplace/
    dd The plugin Claude Code loads.
    dt
      code spool.jsonl
    dd Events waiting to be sent (at most 50 MB).
    dt
      code breaker
      | ,
      code control.json
    dd Small files the hook uses to back off after failures and to answer quickly.
